## Immutable source body: root_degree_D

Historical source retained exactly; current status and interpretation are given above.

# Finite-grid path selection with inverse-polynomial conditioning

Status: proposed root derivation. It composes provisionally on the protected quantitative compiler dd6cca85, whose exposed comparison and acceptance are still pending. No new independent check, scientific program, numerical coefficient example, compiler execution or physical demonstration has occurred. The previous two compiler proofs remain immutable. This is a possible removal of the fixed-certificate finite-window restriction under a further explicit finite polynomial certificate; it does not manufacture any physical supplier.

## 1. Fixed data and a finite polynomial certificate

Keep a fixed finite full plant of dimension d>=2, all old sites, dirty fillers and arbitrary passive reference, the complete computable unchanged H, positive ordinary-NN path J, a fixed port strength g>0 and fixed time unit t0>0. Write the same normalized trace splits H=h0I+Hbar and J=j0I+Jbar. For a real parameter z in [0,1], define the five augmented skew-Hermitian matrices

    X_i(z)=diag(-i t0[Hbar+z Jbar+v_i], -i t0 Hbar),
    v_i in {0,+gX_p,-gX_p,+gY_p,-gY_p}.

Choose s=d^2-1 fixed binary commutator trees T_l, each with leaf count ell_l>=2. Their evaluations are B_l(z)=diag(b_l(z),0). The vanishing second block is algebraic for every z, because all second leaf blocks coincide. In a fixed real Hilbert-Schmidt orthonormal basis of su(d), put the coordinates of b_l(z) in the columns of the real s by s matrix C(z). Every entry is a computable-coefficient polynomial in z, of degree at most ell_l. Thus

    p(z)=det C(z)=sum_(k=0)^D a_k z^k,
    D=sum_l ell_l

is a valid finite degree upper bound; it need not be the exact degree.

The new finite certificate supplies one index k0 in {0,...,D} and a positive rational a with |a_k0|>=a. No assertion that k0 is the first or last nonzero coefficient is required, and no other coefficient is declared zero. This assumption says that this fixed tree family has a nonidentically-zero determinant. It is stronger data than merely knowing controllability at an unspecified coupling, and all construction/search costs of obtaining it are separate from the accuracy bounds below.

One sufficient way to obtain such a certificate is to supply a rational z_star at which this same tree family has a certified nonzero determinant. Then p is not identically zero. Its finitely many computable coefficients can be approximated in parallel at successively finer accuracy until one interval excludes zero; choose a rational positive lower bound on that coefficient. This procedure terminates without deciding which lower coefficients vanish. The qualitative old compiler can motivate finding a tree family and a good z_star, but the present theorem consumes the resulting finite actual certificate. Its discovery time is not a uniform bound in plant size or arbitrary real-oracle complexity.

Supply a rational M>=1 with max_i sup_(0<=z<=1)||X_i(z)||<=M. Such a bound follows from full supplied bounds on Hbar,Jbar,g,t0. Define

    beta_l=2^(ell_l-1) M^(ell_l),
    Bsum=sum_l beta_l,
    ell=max_l ell_l.

These are fixed rational bounds on every ||B_l(z)|| over the whole interval, independent of the later accuracy choice. Supply a positive rational U>=sqrt(d) Bsum. The coordinate matrix then has operator norm <=U: each column has Euclidean norm ||b_l||_F<=sqrt(d) beta_l, and the Frobenius bound on the full matrix is at most sqrt(d) Bsum.

## 2. A finite coupling grid defeats arbitrary lower-order cancellation

For j=0,...,D choose the distinct rational points

    x_j=1/2+j/[2(D+1)].

They lie in [1/2,1). Let V be the fixed rational Vandermonde matrix V_(j,k)=x_j^k, k=0,...,D, and let

    A_V=sum_j |(V^(-1))_(k0,j)| >0.

This is a computable positive rational constant. For any rational 0<Lambda<=1, the vector of values p(Lambda x_j) is V times the coefficient vector (a_k Lambda^k). Therefore

    |a_k0| Lambda^k0
       <= A_V max_j |p(Lambda x_j)|.

Since k0<=D and Lambda<=1, at least one grid value satisfies

    |p(Lambda x_j)| >= a Lambda^D/A_V =: b_Lambda.          (1)

All other coefficients may cancel arbitrarily at individual nodes; the finite interpolation identity prevents cancellation at every node. This uses no smallest-root separation, leading-term dominance, exact zero tests or assumed uniform determinant limit at zero.

At each of the D+1 nodes compute a certified scalar approximation to p with absolute error at most b_Lambda/8. Form the lower bound L_j=|approximation|-b_Lambda/8. Accept a node with L_j>b_Lambda/2. By (1), at least one node has L_j>=3b_Lambda/4 and passes. Use this fixed accuracy for each candidate, so a failed comparison cannot stall indefinitely at a threshold. The selected actual positive rational coupling lambda=Lambda x_j satisfies

    Lambda/2 <= lambda < Lambda,
    |det C(lambda)| > b_Lambda/2.                          (2)

All primitive matrices and every later physical pulse use this actual lambda. The finite coupling search runs before the declared physical preparation begins; actual plant evolution during design needs a separate chronological contract.

## 3. A uniform quantitative basis bound on the selected nodes

All singular values of C(lambda) are at most U. Their product equals |det C(lambda)|. Consequently

    sigma_min(C(lambda)) >= |det C(lambda)|/U^(s-1)
       > a Lambda^D/[2 A_V U^(s-1)].                      (3)

This includes the complete full-plant coordinate matrix; no excitation-sector norm or projected controllability estimate enters. Let K_cert be a fixed rational satisfying

    K_cert >= 1,
    K_cert >= 2 sqrt(sd) A_V U^(s-1)/a.

Then kappa(Lambda)=K_cert Lambda^(-D) is rational and >=1. For any Z=sum_l c_l b_l(lambda) in su(d),

    sum_l |c_l| <= sqrt(s)||c||_2
       <= sqrt(s) ||Z||_F / sigma_min
       <= kappa(Lambda)||Z||.

Thus the quantitative compiler's complete augmented Lie-basis premise holds with a controlled inverse-polynomial conditioning bound, while the same tree list and uniform beta_l remain fixed. It is not inferred from an arbitrary weak coupling: the finite grid selects the node at each actual accuracy.

## 4. Coupling choice for a complete finite output window

Fix a finite supplied window w>=0, a supplied computable full target W with determinant lift theta, a whole-background bound h, and rational b_bar>=||Jbar||, w_bar>=w. Choose a fixed positive rational

    L_win >= max(1,64 b_bar(1+w_bar)).

For any rational desired complete unhalved trace tolerance 0<epsilon<=1, set

    Lambda=epsilon/L_win.

Run the D+1-node selection above, then invoke the quantitative compiler dd6cca85 at its construction tolerance eta=epsilon, with the actual selected lambda, the fixed trees and the rational kappa(Lambda). This is a provisional composition on that exact source, pending its acceptance. Its endpoint/window result, after same-duration C1 positive-port smoothing and restoring physical scalars, is

    ||U_phys(T+s)-e^(-i chi(T+s)) e^(-isH) W e^(-iTH)||
       <=15epsilon/32 + lambda ||Jbar|| w,
    chi(T+s)=(R_s+lambda*j0)(T+s)+A_s+theta,
    0<=s<=w.

The actual returned T includes every positive recurrence wait and the actual H aging. The old H0 remains inside H. The selected coupling satisfies lambda||Jbar||w<=epsilon/64. If the actual full-space generator defect has integrated norm E_int<=epsilon/64 over [0,T+w], the complete joint plant/filler/reference trace error is at most

    2[15epsilon/32+epsilon/64+epsilon/64]=epsilon.          (4)

No output is discarded. The constant scalar reserve must additionally cover any supplied peak defect allowance if robust whole-generator positivity is required. An integrated norm bound alone does not imply that positivity or endpoint work condition.

A fixed nonzero coupling would leave a nonzero window allowance. Equation (4) instead uses a newly selected actual coupling at each accuracy, with its conditioning degradation paid by (3). The determinant coefficient and tree certificate are fixed across the entire family. This is not a claim that every small coupling, or one fixed waveform, works.

## 5. Explicit accuracy powers inherited from the actual recipe

All asymptotics below keep H,J,g,t0,d, the tree list, a,k0,D,U,K_cert and the finite window/target access fixed. The coupling grid is a fixed D+1 candidates; its absolute determinant enclosure tolerance scales as epsilon^D. The scalar coefficient-oracle time needed for those enclosures is not bounded here.

In dd6cca85 the target-grid radius R0=4kappa(d+1), positive coefficient budget K=1+s(R0+2), and all beta_l and Taylor remainder constants depend on the displayed actual inputs. Here beta_l, signed tree lengths and Taylor constants have fixed uniform bounds. In contrast

    kappa=O(epsilon^-D), K=O(epsilon^-D),
    S=K Bsum=O(epsilon^-D),
    C=2K^2 sum_l a_l=O(epsilon^(-2D)).

Use that source's specified sufficient repetition count

    r=ceil max{1,2K,8S^2/epsilon,(16C/epsilon)^ell}.

Do not replace it by an arbitrarily oversized number. With

    R=ell(2D+1),    P=R+2d(R+1),

one obtains

    r=O(epsilon^-R),
    N=O(epsilon^-R),
    Q=O(epsilon^(-(R+1))),
    T=O(epsilon^-P),
    inverse physical ramp width=O(epsilon^(-(R+1))).      (5)

Indeed the first three terms in the maximum grow no faster than epsilon^(-R), while the last has exactly that sufficient power. N is a fixed multiple of r. The recurrence ceiling Q is O(N/epsilon) in the full augmented dimension 2d, and T<=2t0 N Q^(2d). The ramp width formula then gives the last estimate. No actual runtime or pulse example has been measured.

The target-grid spacing in dd6cca85 is proportional to epsilon with a fixed Bsum, while its radius is O(epsilon^-D); hence its number of candidates is O(epsilon^(-(D+1)s)). Each of the five recurrence searches has O(epsilon^(-2d(R+1))) candidates. These are finite candidate counts. They do not bound the classical runtime of arbitrary computable-matrix oracles or the work to discover the certificate.

With a fixed scalar/peak-fault reserve, the full nominal Hamiltonian norm h+R_s+lambda||J||+2g stays uniformly bounded in this external-control family. Static/total norm action has a sufficient O(epsilon^-P) upper bound; pulse variation is O(epsilon^-R), slew O(epsilon^(-(R+1))), and the exact pulse scalar area and static scalar action remain charged. A sufficient constant calibration rate epsilon/[64(T+w)] has inverse allowance O(epsilon^(-(P+1))). That is a precision requirement, not hardware supplied by the proof. Actual control-time error must be charged using the full variation or generator norm and consistent endpoint aging.

The source's regular closed-system endpoint work identities and whole-versus-individual-block positivity qualifications remain unchanged. Physical source installation, pulse actuator energy, cooling/reset and microscopic energy identification are not inferred. Subsequent autonomous clock substitution adds new factors and growing norms; its controller resources and moments require recomputation from the new T and slew rather than automatic inheritance of the bounded external norm.

## 6. Scope, counterexamples avoided and evidence

The interpolation step is valid even if the determinant has a high-order zero at zero or cancellations near some grid points. It does not license an arbitrary lambda. If p is identically zero for the supplied tree family, the nonzero-coefficient certificate fails and this construction says nothing; changing the tree family is a new operation. A known nonzero determinant only at a finite lambda without fixing the same polynomial tree family would not establish (1).

The theorem is conditional on finite complete computable nominal matrices, certified polynomial data and an externally supplied physical path/one-port control/time/isolation interface. Plant size, polynomial degree D and commutator length ell may be enormous; the accuracy exponent is not polynomial in qubit count and no useful numerical constant is asserted. Native memory, clock/basis state, cooling, waveform generation, detector/energy identity, common hardware caps, permanent halt and reuse remain open.

This root proposal was derived after reading the newly protected fixed-certificate compiler dd6cca85 and after writing its own earlier alternative2de387eb. It is not independent of either source or the ongoing campaign. No protected independent derivation of this new interpolation route has been requested or seen. It has no accepted disposition yet. Original proofs and failed substitutions remain unchanged. The selected model/effort was preserved and no physical axiom or editable prompt changed.


